o"],["Shuangbaotai","dish/shuangbaotai"],["Small sausage in large sausage","dish/small-sausage-in-large-sausage"],["Taiwanese beef noodle soup","dish/taiwanese-beef-noodle-soup"],["Taiwanese fried chicken","dish/taiwanese-fried-chicken"],["Taiwanese fried chicken cutlet","dish/taiwanese-fried-chicken-cutlet"],["Tàⁿ-á-mī","dish/ta-a-mi"],["Tea egg","dish/tea-egg"],["Ti-hueh-kué","dish/ti-hueh-kue"],["Tshik-á-mī","dish/tshik-a-mi"],["Tube rice pudding","dish/tube-rice-pudding"],["Xiaolongbao","dish/xiaolongbao"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/64/Pineapple_Pastry.JPG/500px-Pineapple_Pastry.JPG","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Mk2010","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pineapple_Pastry.JPG"}},"dish/pizza-in-new-york-city":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pizza-in-new-york-city","n":"Pizza in New York City","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cuisine of New York City","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Pizza is an important part of the culinary culture of New York City. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, Italian immigrants adapted traditional Neapolitan pizza to the ovens available in New York, leading to New York–style pizza. Pizza of New York then, in turn, influenced pizza-making in Italy through the pizza effect. As of 2018, the city was home to between 800 and 3,000 pizzerias, serving a variety of styles and sometimes in stiff competition with each other. Coal ovens were popular early, gas ovens accompanied the popularity of restaurants selling pizza by the slice, and wood-fired ovens came with the popularity of the Neapolitan style. Slices range from inexpensive dollar slices to gourmet varieties. The price of a slice was once an economic indicator called the Pizza Principle. While the water supply is commonly believed to be responsible for the quality of pizza in the cit"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pizza-in-new-york-city"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pizza_in_New_York_City"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of New York City","cuisine/cuisine-of-new-york-city"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ed/Paulie_Gee%27s_Slice_Shop_%2835386%29.jpg/500px-Paulie_Gee%27s_Slice_Shop_%2835386%29.jpg","w":500,"h":397,"by":"Rhododendrites","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulie_Gee's_Slice_Shop_(35386).jpg"}},"dish/reganao":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/reganao","n":"Regañao","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Aragonese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Regañao is a topped flatbread –similar to a pizza– typical of the Teruel area, in Spain. It has an elongated and rounded shape, and it is covered with salted sardines (sometimes herring) or slices of Teruel ham, all accompanied by strips of red bell pepper that are usually embed in the dough. Regañaos are typical of the Aragonese cuisine, and are often accompanied with wine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-reganao"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rega%C3%B1ao"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Aragonese cuisine","cuisine/aragonese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Piquillo pepper","ingredient/piquillo-pepper"],["Sardines","ingredient/sardines"],["Serrano ham","ingredient/serrano-ham"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]},{"h":"More from Aragonese cuisine","links":[["Cañada bread","dish/canada-bread"],["Chistorra","dish/chistorra"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b4/Rega%C3%B1ao_de_jam%C3%B3n_%28Panader%C3%ADa_Mor%2C_Teruel%29.jpg/500px-Rega%C3%B1ao_de_jam%C3%B3n_%28Panader%C3%ADa_Mor%2C_Teruel%29.jpg","w":500,"h":379,"by":"Panadería Mor","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rega%C3%B1ao_de_jam%C3%B3n_(Panader%C3%ADa_Mor%2C_Teruel).jpg"}},"dish/south-indian-parotta":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/south-indian-parotta","n":"South Indian parotta","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Kerala cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Parotta is a layered Indian flatbread made from maida (refined flour) and oil. It is soft, flakey, and layered. It is commonly seen in Sri Lanka and in South India, especially in the states of Kerala and Tamil Nadu. It is distinct from the North Indian paratha, which uses atta flour."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-south-indian-parotta"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Indian_parotta"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Kerala cuisine","cuisine/kerala-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Yummy Tummy Aarthi (South Indian)","ref":"site/yummytummyaarthi-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"yummytummyaarthi.com","note":"Kerala section of Aarthi's large South Indian home cooking blog.","url":"https://yummytummyaarthi.com/recipes/kerala","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Kerala cuisine","links":[["Chutney","dish/chutney"],["Pundi","dish/pundi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e6/Malabar_Parota_and_egg_curry.JPG/500px-Malabar_Parota_and_egg_curry.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Amanbedi1","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malabar_Parota_and_egg_curry.JPG"}},"cookbook/wookwan-s-korean-temple-food-the-road-to-the-taste-of-enlightenment":{"t":"book","r":"cookbook/wookwan-s-korean-temple-food-the-road-to-the-taste-of-enlightenment","n":"Wookwan's Korean Temple Food: The Road to the Taste of Enlightenment","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Cookbooks","cookbooks"]],"su